  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 149 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nFor this afternoon through tonight, lowly amplified ridge will \nslide across the state bringing WAA and increased moisture aloft\nacross the CWA. Simultaneously, surface low will continue to \norganize over Colorado, while high pressure over southern \nManitoba propagates eastwards into western Ontario. This will \nresult in increasing sky cover at the mid/upper levels this \nafternoon and evening before another round of low-level stratus \nadvects in from the south tonight. Rain is possible tonight with\nthe highest chances south of I94 and also north of Highway 2. \nLow chances remain the forecast elsewhere. A few rumbles of \nthunder are possible in the north, though should be limited in\nareal coverage overall.\n\nBeyond tonight, high pressure over Ontario moves off to the east, \nthough is quickly reinforced by another blocking high. As a result, \nprecipitation chances and rain totals continue backing off for \nmost of the area Friday through this weekend. That said, high \nchances remain in far western North Dakota Saturday afternoon \nthrough Sunday. Forecast rain totals are generally between a \ntenth and quarter inch with locally higher amounts possible. \nMostly dry conditions are then forecast to start the next \nworkweek with low chances returning during the middle of the \nweek. \n\nWith a few exceptions, temperatures are generally forecast to remain \nseasonable over the next week with highs mostly in the mid 60s to \nmid 70s, and lows mostly in the mid 40s to low 50s. See \nweather.gov/bis for the exact temperature forecast at your location. \nOtherwise, a few models indicate that relatively brief, patchy \ndense fog could return tonight mainly in western North Dakota. A\nsoutheasterly wind component and dewpoints remaining in the \nupper 50s support this notion. However, said winds remaining \nbreezy and a potential rain producing stratus deck already in \nplace could result in a failure for fog to develop at all, or at\nleast result in limited visibility reductions.",
